{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3456","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3456","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3456\",\"parent_revision\":\"a40e5aea5ccc7925a4d47ef7073ba44b7017af3e330eaf0427727d61f9d9942a\",\"root_revision\":\"a40e5aea5ccc7925a4d47ef7073ba44b7017af3e330eaf0427727d61f9d9942a\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-09-06\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`find_content_duplicates` scores candidates by how many concept terms appear in their content. `build_concept_query` (`backlog_core/search.py:591`) chooses those terms, capped at four, and reserves half the budget for description-only words taken **in document order** (`candidates = itertools.chain(desc_only_words[:reserved], title_words, ...)`).\\n\\n## Reproduction\\n\\nCreating #3448, whose description opened \\\"Three parts of the dispatch and kage-bunshin path do not work\\\", produced this query:\\n\\n    three OR parts OR dispatched OR wave\\n\\n`three` and `parts` took the reserved half because they are the first two description-only words. Every identifier in the item \u2014 `kage`, `bunshin`, `spawn`, `argparse`, `registry`, `pid` \u2014 was never queried.\\n\\nIt returned five items sharing no subject with the new one: #1859 autonomy modes, #2439 bd prime, #2573 backlog sync redesign, #3062 content identity, #3284 swarm-task-planner commit rules. They matched on \\\"three\\\" and \\\"parts\\\".\\n\\n## The failure runs in both directions\\n\\nFalse positives are visible. False negatives are not: a genuine duplicate about `spawn.py`'s command contract would also have been missed, because `spawn` was never in the query. The conclusion that #3448 was not a duplicate rests on a hand-written search run separately, not on this check.\\n\\n## Measured shape\\n\\nConcept-cap 4 against the same corpus: false-positive candidates all score 0 hits, the true positive scores 3. At cap 12: false positives score 0 or 1, the true positive scores 7. The signal separates cleanly once enough terms are queried \u2014 the terms selected, not the scoring, are what fails.\\n\\n## A noisy gate degrades to no gate\\n\\nA match raises `DuplicateItemError` (`operations.py:1450`) and the only escape is `force=True`, which skips the check **entirely** (`:1444-1445`). Creating #3448 required exactly that. A blocking check that is frequently wrong trains every caller to pass `force=True` by reflex, at which point nothing is checked at all.\\n\\n## One rule, two implementations\\n\\n`work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md` Step 3 separately instructs the agent to run its own concept search before writing. The hand-run version found the genuinely adjacent items (#1047 spawn idempotency, #1061 effort flag); the code version returned the five above. Two implementations of one rule, and the weaker one is the blocking one. Tracked as a class in #3452.\\n\\n## Correction to an appealing but wrong reading\\n\\n`apply_search_filter` (`search.py:298-315`) supports `AND`, `NOT`, parenthetical grouping, regex and field scoping, while Step 3's template uses `OR` alone. That looks like the defect and is not: the hand-written query that outperformed the code was `dispatch_spawn OR spawn.py OR kage-bunshin` \u2014 also `OR`-only. Grammar was never the variable; term selection was. Recorded so grooming does not spend the effort there.\\n\\n## Constraint on where a fix can live\\n\\n`/dh:work-backlog-item --quick` (`references/workflows/quick/start.md:145`) does a selector lookup and calls `backlog add` directly. It never runs Step 3. Any fix placed only in the prose instruction leaves that caller uncovered.\\n\\nOnly two callers invoke the code check: MCP `server.py:1346` and CLI `sam_schema/backlog.py:64`. No scripts and no hooks depend on it.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session audit of backlog_core duplicate detection, 2026-09-06\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-09-06\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Measured Shape\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nConcept-cap 4 against the same corpus: false-positive candidates all score 0 hits, the true positive scores 3. At cap 12: false positives score 0 or 1, the true positive scores 7. The signal separates cleanly once enough terms are queried \u2014 the terms selected, not the scoring, are what fails.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Reproduction\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nCreating #3448, whose description opened \\\"Three parts of the dispatch and kage-bunshin path do not work\\\", produced this query:\\n\\n    three OR parts OR dispatched OR wave\\n\\n`three` and `parts` took the reserved half because they are the first two description-only words. Every identifier in the item \u2014 `kage`, `bunshin`, `spawn`, `argparse`, `registry`, `pid` \u2014 was never queried.\\n\\nIt returned five items sharing no subject with the new one: #1859 autonomy modes, #2439 bd prime, #2573 backlog sync redesign, #3062 content identity, #3284 swarm-task-planner commit rules. They matched on \\\"three\\\" and \\\"parts\\\".\\n</div>\\n\\n## One Rule Two Implementations\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md` Step 3 separately instructs the agent to run its own concept search before writing. The hand-run version found the genuinely adjacent items (#1047 spawn idempotency, #1061 effort flag); the code version returned the five above. Two implementations of one rule, and the weaker one is the blocking one. Tracked as a class in #3452.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Correction To An Appealing But Wrong Reading\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`apply_search_filter` (`search.py:298-315`) supports `AND`, `NOT`, parenthetical grouping, regex and field scoping, while Step 3's template uses `OR` alone. That looks like the defect and is not